  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.824pt, 110.78pt, "The first application envisaged for our TE module, is a failure detection system to detect the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 131.42pt, "deterioration of the thermal insulation of a thermoelectric vacuum insulation panel (TVIP) due ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 152.18pt, "to vacuum loss. As an example, we select the thermal insulation of a battery in a hybrid vehicle ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 172.82pt, "which has a cooling system maintaining the face of the TVIP against the battery at 10°C. The ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 193.58pt, "hot-side temperature of TVIP can be safely assumed to be 50°C due to environmental conditions ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 214.22pt, "(vehicle exposed to the sun) or from engine heating in the case of a hybrid vehicle. The TVIP ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 234.86pt, "has the advantage of limiting the intake of heat coming from the outside and arriving in the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 255.62pt, "cooling circuit dedicated to the battery. Current pressure, force, or temperature based vacuum ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 276.29pt, "sensors require a manual scan [44-46] to recover the data and result in additional mass and costs ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 297.05pt, "than our system. Our system, thus, makes it possible, among other things, to limit the oversizing ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 317.69pt, "of the cooling circuit, which manifests itself in a saving in space and weight, thus extending the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 338.45pt, "autonomy of the battery. It is based on the principle of a decrease in electrical output of a faulty ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 359.09pt, "system upon vacuum loss in TVIP (e.g. due to piercing) as a consequence of a decrease in the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 379.85pt, "thermal conductivity and loss of contact surface from the partial separation of the copper plates ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 400.49pt, "against the RF carbogel material.  ")
  #line-at(76.464pt, 602.95pt, "Figure 4: (A) Loss of vacuum test - Measurement of the output voltage as a function of time ")
  #line-at(80.184pt, 623.74pt, "during a heating cycle, according to three scenarios: a compliant material (TVIP), a failing ")
  #line-at(86.184pt, 644.38pt, "material (loss of vacuum) subjected to a pressure of 3 kPa and a failing material without ")
  #line-at(94.344pt, 665.14pt, "mechanical stress (B) Temperature curves associated with the graph representing the ")
  #line-at(136.46pt, 685.78pt, "evolution of the output voltage as a function of time in figure 4 (A) ")
]

// arXiv source page 10
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, (image("graphics/page-10.png", width: 435pt), 74.5pt, 71.5pt))[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(76.464pt, 245.3pt, "Figure 5: (A) Loss of vacuum test - Measurement of the output voltage as a function of time ")
  #line-at(94.344pt, 265.97pt, "during three heating cycles, when the TEG is under vacuum (TVIP) and subjected to ")
  #line-at(92.184pt, 286.73pt, "mechanical stress (cycle 1), then when the TVIP is pierced and subjected to this same ")
  #line-at(83.904pt, 307.37pt, "mechanical stress (cycle 2) and finally when the TVIP is pierced and without mechanical ")
  #line-at(76.344pt, 328.01pt, "stress (cycle 3) (B) Temperature curves associated with the graph representing the evolution ")
  #line-at(196.97pt, 348.77pt, "of the output voltage as a function of time ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 377.45pt, "This paragraph illustrates a case study with the example of the insulation of a battery for a ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 398.21pt, "hybrid vehicle with the PANOX fibered RF carbogel pyrolyzed at 850°C. A thickness of 2 mm ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 418.85pt, "was finalized for the material after studying the evolution of the surface power as a function of ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 439.63pt, "the thickness of the material for several contact resistance values (see section S2.5 and figure ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 460.27pt, "S9 in the supplementary information). The material being partly intended for thermal insulation ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 481.03pt, "applications, can be assumed to be placed in confined spaces - most often between two walls - ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 501.67pt, "exerting a mechanical stress on the material. Thus, to best represent reality, we also tested the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 522.43pt, "utility of our TVIP to detect vacuum failure for a material subjected to mechanical stress. The ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 543.07pt, "results of this study are shown in figure 4. It is evident that unlike the case study illustrating the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 563.71pt, "example of the insulation of a battery of a hybrid vehicle, we observe here a drastic reduction ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 584.47pt, "of the voltage delivered by the TEG when it loses vacuum. Indeed, for a difference of maximum ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 605.14pt, "temperature observed after 10 minutes of acquisition, the voltage goes from 12 mV in the case ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 625.9pt, "of the material under vacuum, to zero voltage in the case of a failing TEG without mechanical ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 646.54pt, "stress. We also notice that the phenomenon is less pronounced when the material loses vacuum ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 667.3pt, "but remains subject to a mechanical stress of a few kPa. These observations can be explained ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 687.94pt, "by the fact that when the material loses the vacuum, the plastic envelope will tend to swell ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 708.7pt, "slightly which will cause the copper plates to come off; whereas, for TVIP under mechanical ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 729.34pt, "stress, the copper plates are not completely detached and still partially fit the material. Yet, the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 750.096pt, "contact resistance is modified and decreases to around 0.5 mV after 10 minutes of acquisition. ")
]

// arXiv source page 11
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, (image("graphics/page-11.png", width: 452.5pt), 70.5pt, 513.5pt))[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.824pt, 81.96pt, "Following this study, we carried out the same test using the same material, with the aim of ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 102.74pt, "simulating the appearance of a leak occurring in the TVIP. To do this, we started with the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 123.38pt, "material under vacuum constrained by a pressure of a few kPa. We measured the output voltage ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 144.14pt, "during the first heating cycle, then we intentionally pierced the TVIP and measured the output ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 164.78pt, "voltage again during a second heating cycle. Finally, we removed the mechanical constraint ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 185.54pt, "and measured the output voltage one last time during a third heating cycle. We can first notice ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 206.18pt, "that the results of this test, shown in figure 5, are similar to those obtained previously: the TVIP ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 226.94pt, "material subjected to mechanical stress (zone 1) delivers a maximum of approximately 12 mV ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 247.58pt, "during the first heating cycle. Then, when the TVIP is pierced, the voltage drops to ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 268.37pt, "approximately 0.5 mV during the second heating cycle. Finally, when we remove the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 289.01pt, "mechanical constraint, we only recover noise at the output due to the separation of the copper ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 309.77pt, "plates. But the most interesting observation is the evolution of the output voltage when going ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 330.41pt, "from vacuum material to non-vacuum material. In fact, it is at this precise moment that the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 351.17pt, "failure appears. We then notice that at the time of the transition between the TVIP material and ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 371.81pt, "the failing material, the voltage drops suddenly, marking a fairly visible break in the curve. So ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 392.57pt, "visible that an algorithm would be able to identify the appearance of such a phenomenon. Thus, ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 413.21pt, "instead of measuring both the voltage and temperature differences between a vacuum and non-")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 433.99pt, "vacuum material, which can sometimes represent an additional constraint, studying the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 454.63pt, "distribution of measurement points corresponding to the output voltage should suffice. A break ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 475.39pt, "in the curve, or a significant change in slope, materializes the appearance of the failure, hence, ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 496.03pt, "obviating the need for temperature measurements.  ")
  #line-at(82.584pt, 719.5pt, "Figure 6: (A) Picture of a thermoelectric functionalized TVIP, connected to the electronic ")
  #line-at(82.344pt, 740.14pt, "card allowing signal transfer via WiFi (B) HTML interface allowing the visualization and ")
  #line-at(118.82pt, 760.896pt, "recording of data from the TEG, by WiFi connection to the electronic card ")
]

// arXiv source page 12
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, (image("graphics/page-12.png", width: 453.5pt), 71pt, 700pt))[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.824pt, 81.96pt, "It should also be kept in mind that TVIP, on its own, is only a system for converting thermal ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 102.74pt, "energy into electrical energy and is not sufficient to relay output power information indicating ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 123.38pt, "a possible failure. A wireless communication system allowing the transmission of data is ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 144.14pt, "therefore necessary. Figure 6 illustrates such a system equipped with a means of wireless ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 164.78pt, "communication, as part of the thermal insulation of the battery of a hybrid vehicle. For example, ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 185.54pt, "we can imagine that the power signal is relayed to the vehicle dashboard, thus alerting the user ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 206.18pt, "in the event of a vacuum failure. In order to enable data transfer to warn the user of a possible ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 226.94pt, "failure, we used an amplifier system composed of a WiFi antenna, directly connected to the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 247.58pt, "electrical cables at the TVIP output as shown in figure 6.A. An Arduino type electronic card, ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 268.37pt, "operating on a rechargeable battery 1 Ah capacity, requiring 200 mA on continuous use and ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 289.01pt, "few µA on standby was developed to allow for extended use. This electronic card allows the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 309.77pt, "voltage value delivered by the TVIP to be sent, via WiFi, to devices such as computers, ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 330.41pt, "smartphones or tablets. The first step consisted of finding the maximum operating point (MOP) ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 351.17pt, "specific to our material, by modifying the gain value, since our card does not have a MOP ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 371.81pt, "algorithm unlike some other DC-DC converters. This was followed by measurement ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 392.57pt, "repeatability tests consisting of several heating cycles. The results are shown in figures S11 and ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 413.21pt, "S12 and discussed in section S1.7 and S1.8 in the supplementary information (SI), respectively.  ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 441.91pt, "We then also developed the html page with a secure password connection to allow the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 462.67pt, "acquisition, visualization, and download of the measurement curves of the voltage delivered by ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 483.31pt, "the TEG as a function of time as well as to modify the amplifier gain. Thus, after several tests, ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 504.07pt, "we chose to display a voltage value every second, corresponding to the average of 4 ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 524.71pt, "measurement points in one second (one measurement every 250 ms). Figure 6.B illustrates the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 545.47pt, "interface allowing the visualization and recording of data from the thermoelectrically ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 566.11pt, "functionalized TVIP through a device connected via WiFi to the electronic card. In addition, ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 586.87pt, "we have identified another means allowing the detection of vacuum failure. This involves ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 607.54pt, "comparing the expected electrical powers (calculated from model 1 established in section S1.5) ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 628.3pt, "for 1m² of insulation, between a compliant material (TVIP) and a failing material (Figure S13). ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 648.94pt, "The results are presented in figure S13 and discussed in section S1.9 of the SI. To sum up, we ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 669.7pt, "have demonstrated the feasibility of the TVIP system for failure detection: the output voltage ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 690.34pt, "recovered allows us to be informed of a possible loss of vacuum in the thermal insulating ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 711.1pt, "material surrounding a battery. It should be noted that compared to a conventional ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 731.74pt, "thermocouple, the detection by TVIP is spontaneous upon vacuum loss as it immediately alters ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 752.496pt, "the TE properties and causes the copper electrodes to detach thus increasing the contact ")
]

// arXiv source page 13
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, (image("graphics/page-13.png", width: 453.5pt), 71pt, 91.5pt))[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.824pt, 81.96pt, "resistance. A thermocouple will detect the failure by the change in temperature at a given spot ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 102.74pt, "and will therefore be subject to thermal inertia, resulting in a slower detection. Furthermore, ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 123.38pt, "unlike a thermocouple, our wireless self-powered system can be applied in areas where ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 144.14pt, "cables/connections cannot be reliably used. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.824pt, 172.82pt, "2.4 Large scale application and perspectives ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.824pt, 201.5pt, "So far, we only used a module with a single p-type TE element. We consider a series association ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 222.26pt, "of several pyrolyzed and fiberized RF carbogel type materials in PANOX, with the aim of ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 242.9pt, "manufacturing a thermoelectric module with exclusively p-p junctions. The mathematical ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 263.69pt, "models established in S1.5 (SI) to calculate the power delivered by the TEG remain valid, ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 284.33pt, "provided that the thermal conductance, the electrical resistance and the Seebeck coefficient are ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 305.09pt, "multiplied by the number of junctions. Thus, in a second step we defined a “target” material ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 325.73pt, "having the thermoelectric properties that we would like to achieve, i.e. a Seebeck coefficient of ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 346.49pt, "about 50 µV.K")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(141.98pt, 341.93pt, "-1")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(148.7pt, 346.49pt, ", a thermal conductivity of about 30 mW.m")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(353.47pt, 341.93pt, "-1")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(360.19pt, 346.49pt, ".K")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(371.83pt, 341.93pt, "-1")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(378.55pt, 346.49pt, ", and an electrical conductivity ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 367.13pt, "of the order of 10")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(158.66pt, 362.57pt, "3")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(162.74pt, 367.13pt, " S.m")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(185.81pt, 362.57pt, "-1")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(192.41pt, 367.13pt, ". These objectives remain a priori reasonable in view of the current ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 387.89pt, "thermoelectric properties of our material. The main difficulty then consists of obtaining an ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 408.53pt, "electrical conductivity of the order of 10")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(269.09pt, 403.97pt, "3")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(273.17pt, 408.53pt, " Sm")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(292.99pt, 403.97pt, "-1")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(299.71pt, 408.53pt, " since it is currently of the order of 10")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(488.02pt, 403.97pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(492.1pt, 408.53pt, " S.m")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(514.92pt, 403.97pt, "-1")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(521.64pt, 408.53pt, ".  ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 429.29pt, "We, however, have already improved this value by more than 12 orders of magnitude, and, ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 449.95pt, "hence, we can reasonably expect that it is still possible to improve the electrical conductivity ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 470.59pt, "by an order of magnitude more. Furthermore, studies have already revealed electrical ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 491.35pt, "conductivity values of thermoelectric organic materials which can reach several hundred S.m")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(517.8pt, 486.79pt, "-1")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.824pt, 511.99pt, "(or sometimes even 1000 S.m")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(214.25pt, 507.43pt, "-1")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(220.97pt, 511.99pt, ") and having Seebeck coefficient values greater than 50 µV.K")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(517.8pt, 507.43pt, "-1")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.824pt, 532.75pt, "[28, 30-32] – albeit with too high thermal conductivities for a thermal insulation application. ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 553.39pt, "Our unpublished results using reduced-graphene oxide are already promising, though cheaper ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 574.15pt, "conductive fillers must be explored for this optimization. Finally, we defined a second target ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 594.79pt, "material, like the target material stated previously. The only difference is that the latter has a ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 615.58pt, "negative Seebeck coefficient of -50 µV.K")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(286.63pt, 611.02pt, "-1")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(293.35pt, 615.58pt, ". Thus, through these different materials, we ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 636.22pt, "calculated the output voltage expected as well as the power density delivered by a module ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 656.98pt, "composed of p-p junctions with the current material, then p-p junctions with the target material ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 677.62pt, "and finally n-p junctions with the two target materials as a function of the number of junctions ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 698.38pt, "and the temperature difference, while neglecting contact resistances. In each case we considered ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 719.02pt, "the cold side temperature equal to the ambient temperature as well as a thermoelectric module ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 739.78pt, "of 1m² and thickness 2mm. The results of this study are shown in figure 7.  ")
]

