  #line-at(70.824pt, 81.96pt, "due to thermal runaway because of its sensitivity to temperature. It is entirely conceivable to ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 102.74pt, "use this material in other thermal insulation applications under confined spaces. Successful ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 123.38pt, "repeatability proves the durability of the device for long-term use.  ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 152.18pt, "Lastly, the commercial prospect of such a device was explored by extrapolating the power ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 172.82pt, "output and voltage of the material in its current as well as optimized form. We considered the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 193.58pt, "design of a thermoelectric module with junctions, first with exclusively p-p type junctions with ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 214.22pt, "the current material, then with p-p and n-p type junctions with materials having the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 234.86pt, "thermoelectric properties that we foresee to achieve in the future. These properties can be ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 255.62pt, "achieved in various ways, either via the substitution of RF precursors by other types of ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 276.29pt, "electrically conductive organic materials (such as PEDOT: PSS, for example), or by modifying ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 297.05pt, "the synthesis parameters or even by using other types of conductive charges. Such optimization ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 317.69pt, "would then allow access to power density values of several Watts per square meter as well as ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 338.45pt, "output voltage values of several volts for small temperature differences. These values would ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 359.09pt, "therefore be very interesting for room temperature energy storage applications aimed at ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 379.85pt, "powering different remote wireless sensors or auxiliaries [42]. One application can be for ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 400.49pt, "certain difficult to access areas, e.g. as part of the insulation of an aircraft cabin (see figure S15 ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 421.25pt, "in the SI) where space constraints are high with significant constraints in terms of volume and ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 441.91pt, "weight. The little space available therefore has an impact on the technical specificities of the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 462.67pt, "product (thin thickness, low thermal conductivity, etc.). This system can integrate into the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 483.31pt, "required thermal insulation of the aircraft costing almost nothing in terms of weight and volume ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 504.07pt, "whilst providing complimentary applications like standalone sensors for the recovery of flight ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 524.71pt, "data (pressure, temperature, etc.) or thermal comfort through the Peltier effect where large ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 545.47pt, "temperature differences (-50°C exterior / +25°C interior) are at stake.  ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 574.15pt, "Other more general applications, e.g. in cold storage, pipelines, etc. can also be envisaged for ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 594.79pt, "both powering sensors and thermal management. Finally, a CAD study making it possible to ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 615.58pt, "demonstrate the feasibility of assembling such a large module was carried out along with the ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 636.22pt, "successful assembly from an experimental point of view. The key idea we want to convey is ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 656.98pt, "that low power density of carbogel based TE modules can be compensated by flexible large ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 677.62pt, "surface areas whilst their primary use as super-insulating material is further complimented by ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 698.38pt, "thermoelectric power generation.  ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 727.06pt, "The traditional approach has always been to start with a good TE material and then optimize ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 747.696pt, "commercial, environmental, and engineering factors for large-scale ambient heat recovery. ")
  #line-at(70.824pt, 768.456pt, "Contrarily, here we present an alternative strategy of starting with a light thermal insulator, with ")
]

